The IVRS QEMU 11.1.1 published to a q35 with its AMD IOMMU, under QEMU's own
edk2-x86_64-code.fd, with no operating system in the guest: the firmware was
left to reach its shell, and the top 16 MiB of low memory was then written out
by the monitor's `pmemsave`. The RSDP was found in it by its signature and both
checksums and the XSDT walked host-side to the IVRS, so no byte here came
through the decoder these fixtures judge. The table sums to zero.

  qemu-system-x86_64 -nodefaults -machine q35 \
      -cpu qemu64,+rdrand,+smap,+fsgsbase,+x2apic,+smep -smp 2 -m 4G \
      -drive if=pflash,format=raw,unit=0,file=edk2-x86_64-code.fd,readonly=on \
      -drive if=pflash,format=raw,unit=1,file=<a copy of edk2-i386-vars.fd> \
      -device amd-iommu,intremap=off,dma-remap=on -device nec-usb-xhci,id=xhci \
      -vga std -device nvme,serial=deadbeef,id=nvme0ctl,msix-exclusive-bar=on

  ivrs.bin  0x7f775000, 176 bytes, revision 1

`tests/ivrs.rs` lays it under an RSDP and an XSDT of its own; the boot's
XSDT named FACP, APIC, HPET, MCFG, IVRS, WAET and BGRT.
